A||||10||JohnnyK||Taking off that stupid rusted distributor||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-22-2000||03:28 PM||||Well Ogre, you seem to be familiar with this "rusted to the block dist." Any idea how to remedy it? It's REALLY on there.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||EdsB52||08-22-2000||06:30 PM||||Glad to know I'm not the only one! About 2 months ago, I went to remove my distributor and found that it wouldn't spin with the hold-down fork removed. I have never seen this condition before and was looking for another hold-down. Anyway, by the time I was done, I had destroyed that aluminum plate on the top and used a wrench to turn the distributor back and forth. It seems I pried up on the distributor or grabbed it from the top as I turned it back and forth. It was sheer hell. I got the rebuilt distributor from the store to find it wouldn't go in either. I discovered that the distributor hole was too small, so I took a vixen file in one hand, and spun the distibutor with the other to machine it down. GM must have undersized the distributor holes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||08-22-2000||08:29 PM||||So, in other words, I'm screwed?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-22-2000||10:32 PM||||sounds like somone got the wrong part...... either flat out the wrong part Or the wrong part was in the right box which happens allot with rebuild parts.<P>Nope. It's not going to be pretty. Yes, you could destroy the fool thing getting it out. No, there is no easy way to do it.<P>Unless the distributor is bad, leaking oil, or you need to reset timing there really is no need to take it out. I'd put a new cap and rotor on it and leave it in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||08-23-2000||03:24 PM||||Well, the reason I want it off is because the left distributor cap screw will no screw in. I'm considering zip-tying it in there for the kick hill trip, and then fixing it. (Just the left side, it is supported now by the right screw, and it seems to work.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||85SE||08-23-2000||03:49 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Well, the reason I want it off is because the left distributor cap screw will no screw in....it is supported now by the right screw, and it seems to work.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had this problem.  My solution was pretty easy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I got a longer screw and nut and screwed the nut onto the back of the screw, poking the screw through the cap.  Tightened it up and it's as good as if the bracket was working.  I went to the local Canadian Tire, took the screw that came out and got one that eyeballed the same size.  Something says it was a number 6 in 1 and 1/4 inch.  Hope this helps!  It's cheap and easy.  Of course, my distributer is leaking oil from the ring...project #143 with my car   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by 85SE (edited 08-23-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000006||JohnnyK||08-23-2000||04:26 PM||||Explain that again please. I can't make any sense of that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> You took a longer nut and screwed it onto the back of the screw? What?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||85SE||08-25-2000||06:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Explain that again please. I can't make any sense of that.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> You took a longer nut and screwed it onto the back of the screw? What?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi JohnnyK:  Sorry, brain fart I guess  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  What I did was remove the screw from the new cap and took it to the local Canadian Tire (hardware store for you non-Canadians) and matched up the old screw with a longer, yet similar diameter screw and nut.  I slid the new screw through the cap and the bracket on the distributer.  It was a tight fit, but I squeezed my hand in under the bracket and held the little nut so that I could catch it on the screw.  I tighted the screw/net so that the nut was snugged up against the bottom of that bracket and way to go - my cap was as tight as if the screw hole hadn't been stripped.  The screws I used were stainless steel, and the number on the front of the package tells me #6-32x1-1/2"  Price code BB, which is like $1.99 or something for a half dozen.  Spar-pak brand.  Hope this is less confusing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Best regards - Andrew, 85SE<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000008||StuGood||08-27-2000||12:52 PM||||Well, what do you know? I found this thread interesting, because I bought a non-running 1986 SE, and noticed that the <B>left</B> distributor cap screw had been replaced with a (larger-than-OE) self-tapping screw.  Apparently, that stripped left-side hole problem happens more often than I thought! <P>It worked, but it bothered me that, whoever bought the car after I got it going (or their mechanic), would have to reuse that oddball screw whenever they changed the distributor cap    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> .  And use a different socket to tighten each screw.  Who needs that    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> ?!<P>Thought seriously about attaching a nut to the underside of the distributor plate, but then I might have to use a longer screw.  That's no good for me - I wanted to use the stock cap and the screws that came with it.<P>So I blocked the stripped hole with a piece of masking tape underneath the plate.  Next, mixed up a small quantity of epoxy, and daubed it onto the topside of the stripped hole.  The masking tape kept the epoxy from running out until it was set.  Let the adhesive cure until the next day.<P>Next, used the distributor cap as a drill guide and gently drilled through the epoxy, using the tap-drill size for whatever the thread was for the original-equipment distributor cap screws.  Took cap off, countersunk the hole very slightly, and carefully, slowly, tapped threads in the epoxied hole, using an ordinary tap.<P>The whole time I was thinking, "This probably won't work,"  but it did    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !  The threads came out surprisingly strong, and the cap installed perfectly    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ! Don't recall now whether it was "gee whiz high strength," or "five minute" epoxy, but it was clear stuff.<P>At first, I was particularly careful about tightening that left-side screw, but I have had the cap on and off several times since then, and I hardly ever think about it.  Obviously, it pays to take it easy on those distributor cap screws, in any case    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !                 <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 08-27-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000009||theogre||08-27-2000||01:50 PM||||Permatex or someone makes a kit for exactly that use. It uses the screw you want to install as a thread form.||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||08-27-2000||04:21 PM||||I would recommend "JB Weld" for that repair.||||reg||1
